A||||12||CUTTER||CAT. HELP||||5||
Z||000000||CUTTER||12-15-1999||09:25 PM||||Here is a previous reply from Dogtired from another post...<BR>"I pulled the 2 bolts where the exhaust header connects to the rest of the system. And then drove the car (my 87SE), that is how I determined it was my CAT. It is really loud and you don't want to leave it unhooked long, it causes a lot of heat where it shouldn't be, but is relative easy. As much problems as I have had with CATs I would start there. GOOD luck."<P>My CAT is bad and I have no power. How do I take those "2 bolts" off and where are they? <BR>how long can I drive w/ it off for in this 20-30 degree weather?  Any help on how to do this is greatly appreciated!    Thanks, CUTTER||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||12-15-1999||09:35 PM||||Cutter,<P>I think that you misunderstood his comment.  He is telling you how to test and see if your converter is bad.  I do not think that for driving you would want to attempt that as a solution.  It is very possible to melt important parts like engine harness and your decklid by driving for extended periods of time like that.  A good short term solution is to remove the converter, break out the middle section that is plugged (where the exhaust passes the catalyst).  Then re-install the converter.  That way you have removed the obstruction effectively and the vehicle is (illegally) drivable until you can come up with the time or $ to have it fixed.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>You said earlier that you were in WI, right?  If so call Fierodave.  He is located in Mosinee, WI.  I imagine that he knows some shops around the area that could help you out.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||4
Z||000002||CUTTER||12-15-1999||10:14 PM||||I dont know much about cars so how would I do this? if you could tell me step by step it would be great. I have no money what so ever to get a new one so I might do what you said unless it takes alot of time and is not for ametures. The only thing I know how to do is change spark plugs, breaks, plugs, oil change. simple stuff like that.<BR> Thanks, Shane||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-15-1999||11:06 PM||||Jack up the car and crawl under it. If you know what a cat looks like, it'll be very obvious how to remove it. Unfortunately, working on the exhaust is a big pain in the hinny. Rust in the eyes, bolts rusted solid, skinned knuckles when the bolts break off. Using a torch to heat up exhaust bolts helps some. If you don't have a torch (I ain't talking a wimpy propane one here) soak them good with penetrating oil or something similar. Personally, I don't fart around with exhaust work anymore. The little muffler shop I go to is cheap and they do a good job. Saves me a bunch of headaches. Some shops will not remove a cat though, since it is against the law, technically.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||12-16-1999||02:25 AM||||<BR>Yeah... been there, done that. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I agree 100% with everything batboy just said. Cutter, if you can afford gas, you can afford to have a shop remove the cat. Once removed, I guess you could just ram a piece of water pipe or something else rigid through it to unclog it (although I've never done that myself). Then have it put back on. Or have a similar length of exhaust pipe put on in it's place.||||reg||1
